questions.explanation:
N_{2}O_{5(g)} \rightarrow 2NO_{2(g)} + \frac{1}{2} O_{2(g)}
At \(t = 0\): \(P_{0}\) (for \(N_{2}O_{5}\)), \(0\) (for \(NO_{2}\)), \(0\) (for \(O_{2}\))
At \(t = t\): \(P_{0}-x\) (for \(N_{2}O_{5}\)), \(2x\) (for \(NO_{2}\)), \(\frac{x}{2}\) (for \(O_{2}\))
Given that 50% of the reaction is completed, so \(x = \frac{P_{0}}{2}\).
The total pressure at time \(t\) is:
P_{\text{total}} = (P_{0}-x) + 2x + \frac{x}{2}
P_{\text{total}} = P_{0} + x + \frac{x}{2}
Substitute \(x = \frac{P_{0}}{2}\):
P_{\text{total}} = P_{0} + \frac{P_{0}}{2} + \frac{P_{0}}{2 \times 2}
P_{\text{total}} = P_{0} + \frac{P_{0}}{2} + \frac{P_{0}}{4}
P_{\text{total}} = \frac{4P_{0} + 2P_{0} + P_{0}}{4}
P_{\text{total}} = \frac{7P_{0}}{4}
Thus, the final pressure is \(7/4\) times the initial pressure.

